-- title:
--   Invariant chartwise meromorphic pullbacks of sections along the uniformisation
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$. Let $\mathcal O$ be a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), let $\pi \in \mathcal O$ be irreducible (`hπ`), let $\mathcal O$ be $\pi$-adically complete (`hcomplete`), let the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ have exactly $r$ elements (`hres`), and let $(r) = (\pi)$ in $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`); let $K_0$ be a characteristic-zero field which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   Unramified coefficients. $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is a characteristic-zero domain and an $\mathcal O$-algebra equipped with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: completeness of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), maximality of that ideal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo that ideal (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo that ideal (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^r$ modulo that ideal for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   Determinant valuation. $v\!\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is a homomorphism such that $v\!\det(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^n$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$ (`hvdet`).
--
--   The group. $G$ is a group with homomorphisms $\sigma : G \to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and $\rho : G \to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ such that $\rho(g)$ is the class of $\sigma(g)$ (`hρ`), and $\Gamma \le G$ is a subgroup containing an element $z$ with $\sigma(z)$ a scalar matrix and $v\!\det(\sigma z) = 2$ (`hcent`) and an element $w$ with $v\!\det(\sigma w) = 1$ (`hodd`); $\Gamma' \le G$ is characterised by: $x \in \Gamma'$ iff $x \in \Gamma$ and $v\!\det(\sigma x)$ is even (`hΓ'`). On the vertex set of the Bruhat–Tits tree, i.e. on homothety classes of full $\mathcal O$-lattices in $K_0^2$, the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ acts with finite vertex stabilisers (`hdisc`) and with finitely many orbits, witnessed by a finite set of vertices meeting every orbit (`hcocpt`).
--
--   The curve. $\mathcal X$ is a scheme with a proper flat morphism $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, and `hsmooth` asserts that the second projection of the pullback of $f$ along $\operatorname{Spec} K_0 \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$.
--
--   The uniformisation datum $\Theta$. For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, $\Theta$ sends a pair consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ and a Deligne datum over $B$ (an assignment, to each full lattice $M$, of a $B$-submodule of $B \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$ with invertible quotient, monotone for inclusions, equivariant for scalar homotheties, and non-degenerate at every prime of $B$) to a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. Four hypotheses constrain $\Theta$: `hΘnat`, naturality in $B$ for $\mathcal O$-algebra maps; `hΘinv`, invariance under the twisted $\Gamma$-action, namely $\Theta(x') = \Theta(x)$ whenever `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` holds for $\sigma\gamma$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma$, that is whenever the first component of $x'$ is the $\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\!\det(\sigma\gamma)}$-twist of that of $x$ and the Deligne datum of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$; `hΘfib`, stating that for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to k$, every $k$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is $\Theta(\psi, P)$ for some Deligne datum $P$ over $k$, and $\Theta(\psi,P) = \Theta(\psi,P')$ holds precisely when $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ are related by the twisted action of $\sigma\gamma$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma$; and `hΘuniv`, the universality of $\Theta$: for every scheme $T$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho'$ of maps of the same shape satisfying the analogues of `hΘnat` and `hΘinv`, there is a family $u$ of maps from the $\pi$-nilpotent points of $f$ to those of $T$, natural in $B$, with $u \circ \Theta = \rho'$, and every natural family $u'$ with $u' \circ \Theta = \rho'$ coincides with $u$.
--
--   The analytic frame. $C$ is an algebraically closed field, a $K_0$-algebra, complete for a valuation $v$ with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$; $R$ is a commutative ring which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra mapping to $C$ compatibly with the towers $\mathcal O \to R \to C$ and $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$. Here $\varpi$ is a pseudo-uniformiser, i.e. an element of $K_0$ whose image in $C$ has $0 < v(\varpi) < 1$ and such that every nonzero $a \in K_0$ satisfies $v(\varpi)^N \le v(a) \le v(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N$. The hypothesis `hF` (`IsAdicFrame`, seven clauses) requires: $\pi$ irreducible, $R \to C$ injective with image exactly the unit ball $\{v(c) \le 1\}$, $R$ complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, $\mathcal O$ the set of $a \in K_0$ with $v(a) \le 1$, the image of $K_0$ closed in $C$, and $\pi$ and $\varpi$ having the same image in $C$. Further: $\psi_0 : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to R$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra map; `hrk` requires that for all $x, y \in C$ with $v(x) < 1$ and $y \ne 0$ there is $n$ with $v(x)^n \le v(y)$; `hval` requires that every nonzero $\varepsilon \in \Gamma_0$ dominates $v(y)$ for some $y \ne 0$; `hex` (`Omega.IsExhausted`) requires that every point of the Drinfeld upper half plane $\Omega_C = C \setminus K_0$ lies in one of the affinoids $\Omega_n = \{z : v(z) \le v(\varpi)^{-n}$ and $v(z - a) \ge v(\varpi)^{n}$ for all $a \in K_0$ with $v(a) \le v(\varpi)^{-n}\}$; and the ring `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ` of functions on $\Omega_C$ holomorphic on every $\Omega_n$ is assumed to be a domain.
--
--   The uniformisation of points. $\Phi$ assigns to each adic point $x$ of the formal upper half plane over $R$ — a system of Deligne data $x.\mathrm{pt}(n)$ over $R/(\pi^{n+1})$, compatible under the transition maps — an $R$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. The hypothesis `hΦ` has four conjuncts: the reduction of $\Phi(x)$ modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ equals $\Theta$ applied to the reduction of $\psi_0$ together with $x.\mathrm{pt}(n)$, for all $x$ and $n$; $\Phi$ is surjective; $\Phi(x) = \Phi(x')$ if and only if $x' = x.\mathrm{act}(\sigma\gamma)$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma'$; and base change along $R \to C$ is injective on $R$-points of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and hits every $C$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$.
--
--   Finally $s_C : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is the morphism induced by $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$ (`hsC`), and the pullback $\mathcal X_C$ of $f$ along $s_C$ is assumed integral.
--
--   Under these hypotheses, for every open subscheme $U$ of $\mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point of $\mathcal X_C$ and every section $s \in \mathcal O_{\mathcal X_C}(U)$, there exists a function $F : \Omega_C \to C$ with the following four properties.
--
--   First, $F$ computes the values of $s$ along the uniformisation: for every adic point $x$ and every $z \in \Omega_C$ with $z = x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$, and for every morphism $q : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \mathcal X_C$ whose composite with the first projection is the base change along $R \to C$ of $\Phi(x)$ and whose composite with the second projection is the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} C$, and for every witness that $q^{-1}U$ is the whole of $\operatorname{Spec} C$, the value $F(z)$ is the image of $s$ under $q$ at $U$, restricted to the total open set and transported through the isomorphism $\mathcal O_{\operatorname{Spec} C}(\top) \cong C$.
--
--   Second, $F$ is $\Gamma'$-invariant: for every $\gamma \in \Gamma'$ and all $z, w \in \Omega_C$ with $w = \rho(\gamma)z$ (the Möbius action of $\rho(\gamma)$ on $C$ through the one-point extension), $F(w) = F(z)$.
--
--   Third, $F$ is meromorphic on each affinoid chart: for every $n$ there are functions $a, b$ on $\Omega_n$, both holomorphic on $\Omega_n$ in the sense of `Omega.holOn` (uniform limits of uniformly bounded sequences of rational functions without poles on $\Omega_n$), with $b$ not identically zero on $\Omega_n$, such that $b(z)F(z) = a(z)$ for every $z \in \Omega_n$ with $b(z) \ne 0$.
--
--   Fourth, $F$ is meromorphic with non-vanishing denominator at each uniformised point lying over $U$: for every adic point $x$ and $z \in \Omega_C$ with $z = x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$, and every $q$ as in the first property with $q^{-1}U$ the whole of $\operatorname{Spec} C$, there are an integer $N$, a witness that the closed disc $D = \{w \in C : v(w - z) \le v(\varpi)^N\}$ is contained in $\Omega_C$, and functions $a, b$ on $D$, both holomorphic on $D$ in the above sense, such that $b(w) \ne 0$ for every $w \in D$ equal to $z$, and $b(w)F(w) = a(w)$ for every $w \in D$.
--
--   This is the analytic half of the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison: it transports a rational function on the integral curve $\mathcal X_C$ to a $\Gamma'$-invariant function on Drinfeld's upper half plane which is meromorphic on every affinoid chart and has a non-vanishing denominator at each uniformised point. It is used in the proof that the function field of $\mathcal X_C$ maps onto the field of $\Gamma'$-invariant chartwise meromorphic functions, the step that identifies the quotient of the upper half plane with the curve.
